WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.640
have you ever thought about where your leftover&nbsp;
food goes after you throw it in the trash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.040
or the consequences that it has for our planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.680
in the u.s alone about 37 million tons of&nbsp;
discarded food were brought to landfills in 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.120
that's equal to the weight of 100 empire state&nbsp;
buildings in landfills food scraps and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.680
organic material are broken down by bacteria in&nbsp;
the absence of oxygen which produces methane a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.520
potent greenhouse gas with even greater capacity&nbsp;
to trap heat in our atmosphere than carbon dioxide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.680
composting is an eco-friendly alternative to&nbsp;
discarding our food waste with three important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.840
advantages first composting allows food scraps&nbsp;
to degrade in the presence of oxygen meaning no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.800
methane is produced second composting mimics&nbsp;
natural nutrient recycling processes breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.560
down biodegradable materials to produce organic&nbsp;
fertilizer a sustainable alternative to synthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:09.680
fertilizers that require high amounts of fossil&nbsp;
fuel energy to produce third composting diverts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.560
waste from landfills preserving natural ecosystems&nbsp;
that would otherwise be cleared for landfill use

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.160
and the best part you can&nbsp;
compost in your own backyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.840
click the link in the description to learn more

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.420
you

